WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.080
Good morning, John.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:05.180
I had pretty much the worst anxiety dream
of all time last night. It's pretty horrible

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:08.250
- I don't really know if I want to share it
with you, but I guess I'm going to because

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.750
that's what I want this video to be about,
is anxiety.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:16.840
So yeah, this is bad, prepare yourself: I
dreamt that I was a pilot of a commercial

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:22.849
jet and I crashed it - me, I did it, it was
my fault - and then I had to go to all the

00:00:22.849 --> 00:00:28.050
funerals of the people who died and talk to
their families and look at their coffins.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:33.710
Give me "showing up to class in my underpants"
any day; I will take "didn't go to class all

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:37.870
semester long and now I have to take the final,"
sure. I actually still have those. If you're

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:40.180
in school and you're like, "Oh, I can't wait
to get out of school so I can stop having

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:44.530
these school-based anxiety dreams," that never
happens. You will always dream that you didn't

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:49.360
go to class all semester long and now you
have to go, uh, take the final. That will

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.110
always happen to you. Forever. Until you die.

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.430
Our brains are stupid.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:58.150
Turns out, according to a landmark study that
was completed in 1985, anxiety is the most

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:02.390
common emotion felt in dreams. Which makes
me wonder whether or not anxiety is, in fact,

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:07.770
the most common emotion felt total. I'm feeling
like, right now, anxiety is my most common

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:08.770
emotion.

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:12.220
There's a lot going on for me right now. I'm
about to finish my album, Ellen Hardcastle.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:16.030
I'm very excited... but also scared. I've
been working on it for a long time and I'm

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:19.720
really emotionally invested in it. There's
a lot of my, like, time and passion and energy

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:22.940
have gone into it, and I know that people
aren't going to hate it, 'cause it's good,

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:27.050
like, I know people are going to like it,
but that's what I know. What I feel is that

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:29.710
everyone is going to hate it, and they're
all going to leave me comments saying "Why

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:33.310
did you release this piece of crap out into
the world? You are such a bad person!"

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:38.930
Also, I'm making a video with NASA, which
is just like, "What?!" All the moments of

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:42.930
my life where I'm not thinking, "Oh my gosh,
this is so cool," I'm thinking, "Oh my god,

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:43.990
don't screw up."

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:48.300
I'm officiating a wedding this weekend for
a couple of my best friends, so yeah. My rational

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:53.190
side is like, "Oh my god, what an honor, you
guys really like me that much?" But my irrational

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:58.790
side, all it heard was, "Hey Hank, do you
want to, uh, not screw up the most important

00:01:58.790 --> 00:01:59.920
moment of our lives?"

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:03.700
And I'm playing a show with Harry and the
Potters tomorrow night. Playing shows is always

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:08.170
scary, more scary when you're playing it with
the coolest people ever. If you live in Missoula,

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:09.700
you should come to that. There's a link.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:12.760
And then of course you have the most stressful
thing in my life, which is VidCon. There's

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:18.170
ten thousand things at VidCon, let's just
assume, around ten thousand. Failure rate

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:22.980
of one percent: super good. But when you've
got ten thousand things, it means that some

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:27.400
things are gonna break and go wrong and fail.
And when I'm me and I'm looking, all I see

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:31.540
is the little fail-y things, and I think that's
all that everybody's gonna see. But of course

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:34.450
what everybody sees is the 9,900 things that
went really right.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:39.560
And yeah, I know most of you are like, "Shut
up about VidCon! I can't go, and it's already

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:43.450
sold out." But, uhhh, next year, 2012, we
are going to have a much bigger venue.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:46.270
So all this made me realize that doing stuff
is really hard, especially when you're doing

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:50.350
it for other people and you care what they
think about you. Which is why I think that

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:54.770
there are so many sociopaths and psychopaths
in positions of power: because those people

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:58.830
don't care about other people. So when they
do these big, important things, they're not

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:02.500
freaking stressed out as hell, like ripping
their frickin' faces off 'cause they're so

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:07.320
scared. Because they don't care, because they're
psychopaths! And then we're shocked when they

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:12.900
send pictures of their penis to random interns.
No! We set the system up for this. It's way

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:16.319
too scary to be in a position of power, unless
you're a psychopath.

00:03:16.319 --> 00:03:20.280
So that worries me. Like if you want to do
big, cool, interesting things, either you

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.599
have to be a psychopath, or you have to be
really stressed out. And I don't want to be

00:03:22.599 --> 00:03:24.910
either of those things, but I also don't want
to stop doing cool stuff.

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:28.190
Sorry if I'm opening the window of my soul
here, but now I'm going to tell you why it's

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:34.060
different for me and how rare this is, that
the people I'm doing stuff for, they care

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:39.430
about me and they're understanding. And wow
- that's a very unique and awesome gift and

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:43.770
I thank you all for it. And I also thank John
for it, for making me stronger without freakin'

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:46.030
stressing me out, which he never does.

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:52.150
Speaking of which - he's not feeling well,
so everyone send warm wishes to John. Uh,

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:57.050
he's got some stomach problems, which is why
he has not eaten his sandwiches yet. So John,

00:03:57.050 --> 00:03:58.550
feel better, and I will see you on Wednesday.

